<div class="learn-content"><section class="article-section" id="meaning"><p class="eyebrow">01 / THE MEANING</p><h2>A name with <em>depth.</em></h2><p class="lead">Sanatana <a class="glossary-link" href="/glossary.html#dharma" title="dharma: glossary definition">Dharma</a> is a name used for Hinduism. It is often rendered as “eternal dharma” or “the eternal way.”</p><p><strong>Sanatana</strong> conveys enduring or eternal. <strong>Dharma</strong> has a broad range of meanings, including ethical responsibility, right conduct and the principles that sustain life. Translating it as only “religion” or only “duty” leaves much of its meaning behind. </p><p>Hinduism brings together sacred literature, philosophical inquiry, devotion, ritual and community traditions. It has no single historical founder or one book that alone represents the whole tradition. </p><div class="reader-note"><span>KEEP IN MIND</span><p>This guide introduces shared themes. A particular school, text or community may explain them differently.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-section" id="ideas"><p class="eyebrow">02 / FOUNDATIONAL IDEAS</p><h2>Six ideas to <em>begin with.</em></h2><p>These terms recur throughout Hindu teaching. Treat the short explanations as entrances into larger discussions.  </p><div class="idea-grid"><article><span lang="sa">धर्म</span><h3>Dharma</h3><p>Ethical responsibility and a way of living that supports spiritual growth. What right action requires depends on the situation.</p></article><article><span lang="sa">कर्म</span><h3>Karma</h3><p>Action and its consequences. The teaching connects action with the unfolding of experience and spiritual development.</p></article><article><span lang="sa">संसार</span><h3>Samsara</h3><p>The cycle of birth, death and rebirth, from which spiritual liberation is sought.</p></article><article><span lang="sa">मोक्ष</span><h3>Moksha</h3><p>Liberation from samsara. Traditions differ in how they describe liberation and the path toward it.</p></article><article><span lang="sa">आत्मन्</span><h3>Atman</h3><p>The self or spiritual essence. Inquiry into its nature is central to many Hindu teachings.</p></article><article><span lang="sa">ब्रह्मन्</span><h3>Brahman</h3><p>Ultimate reality in Hindu philosophical discourse. Its relationship to the self and the world is understood differently across schools.</p></article></div><p class="term-note"><strong>A useful distinction:</strong> <a class="glossary-link" href="/glossary.html#brahman" title="brahman: glossary definition">Brahman</a>, a philosophical term for ultimate reality, is different from Brahma, the deity associated with creation. </p></section>
<section class="article-section" id="paths"><p class="eyebrow">03 / SPIRITUAL PATHS</p><h2>Different ways to <em>seek.</em></h2><p>Devotion, knowledge, selfless action and meditation are commonly presented as spiritual paths. They can complement one another, and their emphasis varies across traditions. </p><div class="path-list"><article><span>01</span><div><h3>Bhakti · Devotion</h3><p>Cultivating love for the Divine through worship, prayer and remembrance.</p></div></article><article><span>02</span><div><h3>Jnana · Knowledge</h3><p>Study, reflection and inquiry into the self and reality.</p></div></article><article><span>03</span><div><h3>Karma yoga · Selfless action</h3><p>Acting responsibly without making personal reward the centre of every action.</p></div></article><article><span>04</span><div><h3>Dhyana · Meditation</h3><p>Training attention through contemplative practice. Introductory accounts also use the term raja yoga; terminology varies.</p></div></article></div><div class="scripture-note"><p class="eyebrow">A SCRIPTURAL CONNECTION</p><h3>Action without attachment</h3><p>Bhagavad Gita 2.47 teaches engagement in action without attachment to its fruits, while also cautioning against attachment to inaction.</p><a href="https://www.gitasupersite.iitk.ac.in/srimad?language=dv&amp;field_chapter_value=2&amp;field_nsutra_value=47&amp;etsiva=1" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Read verse 2.47 at IIT Kanpur’s Gita Supersite</a><small>Paraphrase of the verse; not a direct translation.</small></div></section>
<section class="article-section" id="diversity"><p class="eyebrow">04 / A FAMILY OF TRADITIONS</p><h2>Shared roots.<br/><em>Distinct perspectives.</em></h2><p>Shaiva traditions centre Shiva; Vaishnava traditions centre Vishnu and his forms; Shakta traditions centre the Divine Mother. Smarta traditions honour multiple deities. These broad groupings contain many lineages and local expressions. </p><p>Philosophical schools also offer different perspectives. Nyaya, Vaisheshika, Samkhya, Yoga, Mimamsa and Vedanta are conventionally listed as six major darshanas. Vedanta itself includes differing schools such as Advaita, Vishishtadvaita and Dvaita. </p><div class="reader-note"><span>READ WITH CONTEXT</span><p>A teaching from one lineage should be identified as that lineage’s view. It need not speak for every Hindu tradition.</p></div></section>
<section class="article-section" id="living"><p class="eyebrow">05 / A LIVING HERITAGE</p><h2>Beyond the <em>page.</em></h2><p>The tradition lives through homes and temples, teachers and communities, prayer, meditation, festivals and pilgrimage. Practice varies by family, region and lineage. </p><p>Sacred literature spans many forms: the four Vedas, the philosophical Upanishads, the Ramayana and Mahabharata, and the Puranas. The Bhagavad Gita is a dialogue within the Mahabharata. These genres offer different contexts for understanding a teaching or story. </p><div class="context-prompt"><h3>When you read a story, ask:</h3><ul><li>Which text or tradition does it come from?</li><li>Is this a translation, a retelling or a regional version?</li><li>What question about life does it invite me to consider?</li></ul></div></section>
<section class="article-section faq" id="questions"><p class="eyebrow">06 / COMMON QUESTIONS</p><h2>Start with a <em>question.</em></h2><details><summary>Is Sanatan Dharma different from Hinduism?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>Sanatana Dharma is commonly used as a name for Hinduism. It emphasises an enduring spiritual way, while “Hinduism” is the widely used name for this diverse family of traditions. </p></details><details><summary>Does everyone worship the same deity?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No. Different traditions centre different deities and understand the Divine through different theological and philosophical perspectives.  <a href="/deities.html">Explore gods and goddesses.</a></p></details><details><summary>Is there one sacred book?<span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><p>No single book represents all Hindu traditions. The Vedas, Upanishads, epics, Puranas and other texts have different roles; particular communities place special emphasis on particular works. </p></details></section>
